---
// The one piece of arithmetic that gets a section to itself: the trim is
// charged where it happens, and it moves the QUANTITY as well as the money.
//
// COMPRESSED 2026-08-21 from the 234-line version that sat unimported. That
// version argued three layers deep (you set it / it offers a USDA reference /
// it learns from what you buy) plus a five-rule refusals panel. Two reasons
// only layer one survived:
//
//   1. One claim per section. The page is a billboard; the depth belongs on
//      /features, which is what /features is for.
//   2. TRUTH PASS. docs/release-claim-ledger.md backs the yield-on-the-line
//      claim at RC-16 (ingredients carry yield and conversions) and RC-19
//      (recipe lines show used, yield, to-buy, prep and cost contribution).
//      It does NOT carry a row for the USDA reference chip or for learned
//      yield. Those claims came off the page rather than onto the ledger.
//
// Read off the code, verified on develop 2026-08-02 and unchanged:
//   core/engine.ts, ingredientLineQty()
//     `ap = ep / yieldFraction(line.yieldPct, recipeName)`, and its comment
//     says both expansion and costing call it "so yield/conversion behavior
//     can never diverge between them". That is the load-bearing claim here:
//     the yield moves the quantity, not just the money. A tool that gets the
//     cost right and the quantity wrong still leaves you short at 5am.
//   server/db/schema.ts:114
//     `defaultYieldPct: real('default_yield_pct').notNull().default(100)`.
//     Nothing is assumed until a human sets it, which is why the copy says so
//     rather than implying a stocked database.
import SectionHandoff from '../SectionHandoff.astro';
---

<section id="yield" class="container-page border-t border-hairline py-section">
	<div class="max-w-2xl" data-reveal>
		<p class="eyebrow">The hard part</p>
		<h2 class="mt-4 text-h2 font-semibold">The case has the skins on.</h2>
		<p class="mt-5 text-lede text-ink-soft">
			Your recipe says five pounds of peeled shallots. The case you buy is five pounds
			eleven ounces with the skins on, and you paid for the skins.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-5 text-ink-soft">
			Most sheets add ten percent for waste at the end. Right on average, wrong on every
			line, because shallots do not behave like a case of romaine. Set the yield on the
			recipe line instead and two things move together, what the
			plate costs and how much you buy. The same function computes both, so they cannot
			drift. Every ingredient starts at 100 percent until you say otherwise.
		</p>
	</div>

	<!-- The screenshot leads rather than illustrates: three columns in one row
	     make the argument faster than the paragraph above can, and a reader who
	     stops here has still got the point.

	     RECAPTURED 2026-08-21 from the same demo world and the same capture run
	     as the homepage video, so a reader who watches the film and then studies
	     this figure sees one kitchen and one set of numbers. The pair that was
	     here before came from a seed generation that no longer exists, which is
	     also why it was three weeks staler than everything else in public/proof:
	     the app repo's @landing test regenerated every other crop and not this
	     one. It produces `yield-lines` now. See scripts/demo-video/README.md.

	     ALT TEXT IS A CLAIM, NOT A LABEL. It is read in place of the image, so
	     it is prospect-facing copy under every rule in CLAUDE.md and a truth-pass
	     surface. If the capture moves, it is rewritten from the new image, along
	     with the figcaption and the width/height attributes. -->
	<figure class="mt-10" data-reveal>
		{/* The phone figure is ONE line, not a crop of the table. Below 960px the
		    app stops rendering these as a table at all and gives each line its own
		    card, so there is no cost column to shear (the old failure: at 390 a
		    full-width capture sheared "$0.33" into "$0.3" with the word "cost"
		    running down the side). Roma tomato because it is the line where the
		    argument is plainest: 60 g in the pot, 66 g on the list. */}
		<img
			src="/proof/yield-lines-mobile.png"
			alt="One recipe line on a phone, with its calculation open. Roma tomato, prep cored and wedged, $0.17. Used in recipe 60 grams. Trim yield 91 percent. Required to buy 66 grams. Share of plate cost 12 percent. View line calculation: used in recipe 60 grams usable quantity; trim yield 91 percent; required as purchased 60 grams divided by 0.91 equals 66 grams; current purchase cost, 25 pound case at $29.14 per 25 pounds, $2.57 a kilogram; line cost $0.17, 12 percent of the recipe-line subtotal. Recorded purchase, supplier Green Valley Produce, effective 2026-07-28."
			width={712}
			height={1112}
			loading="lazy"
			decoding="async"
			class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-[356px] border border-hairline bg-paper shadow-ticket sm:hidden"
		/>
		{/* 1136px is 1:1 for a 2x capture. The width/height attributes carry the FILE
		    size, not the CSS size. (This comment lives outside the attribute list on
		    purpose: Astro parses a block comment placed among attributes AS
		    attributes.) */}
		<img
			src="/proof/yield-lines.png"
			alt="Three lines of a recipe table with columns for ingredient, used, yield, to buy (AP), prep and cost. Bell pepper: 0.25 each used at 82 percent yield, 0.3 each to buy, seeded and large diced, $0.33, 23 percent of plate cost. Cucumber: 0.3 each at 90 percent, 0.3 each to buy, peeled into half-moons, $0.38, 27 percent. Roma tomato, with View line calculation open: 60 grams used, 91 percent yield, 66 grams to buy, cored and wedged, $0.17, 12 percent. The calculation reads: used in recipe 60 grams usable quantity; trim yield 91 percent; required as purchased 60 grams divided by 0.91 equals 66 grams; current purchase cost, 25 pound case at $29.14 per 25 pounds, $2.57 a kilogram; line cost $0.17, 12 percent of the recipe-line subtotal; recorded purchase from Green Valley Produce, effective 2026-07-28."
			width={2268}
			height={1270}
			loading="lazy"
			decoding="async"
			style="width: 1134px; max-width: 100%"
			class="hidden h-auto border border-hairline bg-paper shadow-ticket sm:block"
		/>
		{/* "Three columns in one row" was the old caption and it is only true of the
		    desktop figure. Below sm: the figure is one card, and a caption naming
		    columns that are not there is the kind of small lie a reader on a phone
		    catches instantly. The line reads correctly under both. */}
		<figcaption class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-ink-soft">
			Used, yield, bought. The pot needs 60 grams of tomato, the tomato gives up
			91 percent of itself, so 66 grams goes on the list. Small on one plate, a case
			at two hundred.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<SectionHandoff from="yield" />
</section>
